{
 "_comment": "NSReg (ICLR 2025) as the second reproduced baseline, under OUTPOST's protocol.\n\nWHY. Every head-to-head in the paper is OUTPOST vs DEMO. One reproduced\nbaseline is a standard reject line, and the paper's strongest claim - that\nrankings change under the honest selection rule - is currently a two-method\nclaim. NSReg is the paper that defined this benchmark protocol; with it the\nranking-flip test has three methods and the DEMO reproduction-gap analysis\nhas a second instance.\n\nARMS.\n  E400_nsreg  400 epochs, the paper's uniform budget (what DEMO is granted).\n              10 seeds on all five paired datasets = 170 rotation-jobs.\n  N201_nsreg  201 epochs, NSReg's own released config, 5 seeds on the four\n              datasets they published: the reproduction check. If our\n              NSReg lands well below their Photo 0.836 / Computers 0.740 /\n              CS 0.903 / Yelp 0.702, that is reported as a gap exactly as\n              DEMO's was, not hidden.\n\nIDENTICAL TO OUTPOST BY CONSTRUCTION: graph tensors (utils.load_data), the\nper-(seed,rotation) split (same function object after the same set_seed\ncall), metric definitions, selection rules, and shard format. See\nbaselines/run_nsreg.py. The split hash is stored in every shard.\n\nNSREG'S OWN: everything about the model. Only the mag_cs configuration was\nreleased; it is used for every dataset with input_dim changed. Stated as a\nlimitation.\n\nCORRECTION 2026-09-04: cs jobs carried mem_gb=20, overriding the 45 GB\ntable entry, so the scheduler packed two CS jobs per card and the second OOMed\nonce the first's allocator grew (35 GB reserved against 23 GB allocated). Set\nto 45 - one per card - and the wrapper now caps its own reservable memory at\n0.58 of the card (NSREG_MEM_FRACTION). Finished jobs are skipped on resume.",
